+- **What changes**: `GET /api/getPermUserInfor` and `POST /syncAndReplaceTabs` used to depend only on your `verifyAppUser`. Now this package also requires: (a) a caller that is in a row of the `users` table (found by `mappingBy`, active or not; an application token bound to such a user counts) is judged by that row — active with `isAdmin: 'y'`; (b) a caller that is in no row of the table is accepted when the `getUserByToken` result reports `isAdmin: 'y'`, and **nothing else is checked there**: `isApp` need not be forwarded and the `id` need not start with `app:`. This is compatible by design: the application identity of w-web-sso up to now was simply `isAdmin: 'y'`, so **a deployment whose application identities are in no row of `users` and that did not touch `getUserByToken` keeps working after the upgrade** (an identity that matches a row of `users` is judged by that row, whatever `getUserByToken` reports). Refused with `userNotAdmin`: an ordinary user (a row with `isAdmin: 'n'`), an application token bound to an ordinary user, and an identity that is not in the table and whose `isAdmin` is not `'y'`. A caller found in a disabled row is refused by the row (`cannotGetUserFromPerm`). An administrator that exists only upstream (in no row of `users`) is accepted with its own user token and, equally, with an application token bound to it. Your `verifyAppUser` is still called first and can only tighten.
 - **What to do**: nothing is required for compatibility. Recommended: make your `getUserByToken` return the optional field `isApp` (`'y'` / `'n'`; missing = `'n'`) exactly as the upstream lookup (w-web-sso) reports it, because the system-service purpose below needs it. Your `verifyAppUser` no longer has to check `isAdmin`; the old example (`return user.isAdmin === 'y'`) and the new one (`return true`) both work, but the old one hides the difference between `userNotAdmin` and `userNoPermission`.
 - **The system-service purpose on the console login check and the data channel (new; stricter than the application routes)**: these entries (the console login check, every data-channel entry, the four write functions) accept an identity that is in no row of `users` only when **all four** hold: your `getUserByToken` result has `isApp: 'y'`, `isAdmin: 'y'` and an `id` **starting with `app:`** (w-web-sso returns `app:<key id>` for the application key itself and refuses user ids with that prefix). It goes through `verifyAppUser` (not `verifyClientUser`) and the audit columns record its id (`app:...`). **Your `getUserByToken` must forward `isApp` for this** (the bundled `srv.mjs` shows the test tokens `{token-for-app-system}` etc.). An application token bound to an administrator that exists only upstream reports that user's own id (no `app:` prefix), so these entries refuse it exactly like the administrator's own user token; the same holds for an identity with `isApp: 'y'`, `isAdmin: 'y'` and an `id` without the prefix. These entries used to refuse every identity that is not in the `users` table, so nothing that worked before stops working.
 - **What "in the `users` table" means**: the identity found by `mappingBy` (active or not) **or** whose `id` equals the primary key of any row of `users`. An identity that is in the table is always judged by that row (so an application token whose `id` happens to be the key of a user row is never a system-service identity, whatever else it reports); only an identity that is in no row is a candidate for the system-service purpose or for rule (b) above. Your `getUserByToken` result is copied once per request, so changing the object after returning it has no effect.
 - **Audit columns written through `/syncAndReplaceTabs` (behavior change for identities found in `users`)**: they now record the `id` of the caller's `users` row, exactly as the data channel does (an application token bound to a user records the bound user's `id`). Before, they recorded the `id` your `getUserByToken` reported, which differs from the row's `id` when `mappingBy` is `'email'` or `'name'` or the caller is an externally imported user (found by `srcKey`); with the default `mappingBy: 'id'` and users created in the admin pages the two are the same. A caller that is in no row records the `id` your `getUserByToken` reported (`app:...` for the system-service purpose).
-- **Check before upgrading**: count srLog `getAndVerifyAppUser-error` with `msg: 'userNotAdmin'` after the upgrade; each is an application call whose `getUserByToken` result does not report `isAdmin: 'y'` (and that is not in `users` as an administrator).
+- **Check before upgrading**: count srLog `getAndVerifyAppUser-error` with `msg: 'userNotAdmin'` after the upgrade; each is an application call that is refused because the caller matches a `users` row that is not an active administrator (the row decides even when your `getUserByToken` reports `isAdmin: 'y'`), or because the caller is in no row and your `getUserByToken` result does not report `isAdmin: 'y'`.
 
 **2. Console-login screen and the example `verifyClientUser` (behavior of the bundled example)**
 - The bundled `srv.mjs` no longer vetoes ordinary users in `verifyClientUser` (it used to return `user.isAdmin === 'y'`, which sent an ordinary user to the generic "Login denied" screen and a redirect). It returns `true`, so an ordinary user is refused by this package with `userNotAdmin` and always sees "No permission to enter the permission console". A veto from your own `verifyClientUser` (for a reason other than the role — source network, system) answers `userNoPermission`, shows "Login denied" and redirects to `opt.urlRedirect` (after 60 seconds in development), as before.
